      "role": "user",
      "content": "i've been conducting a spatial mapping of the acoustic output near the municipal building using a phased array of infrasonic transducers. the data is extremely specific: i'm seeing highly concentrated standing wave nodes at 13.5Hz, particularly in the north plaza seating area, with peak amplitudes hitting 84dB. \n\nwhat's strange is the phase-shifting; it looks like the signal is being modulated to create these specific \"hot zones\" of constructive interference in areas where people tend to linger. if this were just faulty infrastructure or HVAC, the distribution would be much more diffuse and random. given the precision of these spatial concentrations and the specific frequency, what kind of technical intent would drive the deployment of a signal designed to create localized zones of high-intensity infrasonic resonance in a public space?",
